Snoudona can be questioned in Russia
The former employee of American special services Edward Snowden, most likely, will not go outside of Russia to testify in cases of wiretapped telephone conversations of German Chancellor Angela Merkel.
As told “Interfax” on Friday a source familiar with the situation, the American can answer the questions of representatives of the Prosecutor General’s office of Germany in writing or in person in Russia.
The departure of Edward Snowden outside of Russia is practically eliminated.
“In this case, he will lose refugee status and can be extradited to Washington by U.S. allied countries,” the source said.
“At the same time, in principle, the Prosecutor General’s office of Germany could send its representatives to the Russian Federation or convey written questions to Snowden,” the source added, adding that this issue should be worked out at the interstate level.
Earlier, the Deputy of the German Bundestag Hans-Christian Strebele said that Edward Snowden is ready to testify in the case of wiretapping by American special special services of negotiations of the German chancellor.
Snuden is ready to testify to the German law enforcement authorities either in Moscow or in Germany.
An ex-CIA officer made it clear that he knows a lot.
Despite the fact that the U.S. NSA blocked all its movements, Snowden is ready to come to Germany.
However, for this it is Necessary to clearly identify all the conditions of its arrival," Strebrelet told Euronews.
Snowden’s lawyer Anatoly Kucherena, commenting on an Interfax source statement, said that his client can cooperate with German Investigators, but when and how - will decide for itself.
U.S. citizen Edward Snowden, who worked for the National Security Agency (NBS) of the United States and the CIA, fled to Hong Kong in May 2013, after which he published information about the secret U.S. security operations on Internet surveillance.
Snuden flew through Moscow in transit, but could not leave the airport due to the fact that his passport was announced by the U.S. authorities in absentia, which in absentia filed serious accusing him, and until the beginning of August remained in the transit zone of Sheremetyevo airport.
On the first of August, Snowden received a document granting him a temporary asylum in Russia for a period of a one-after one year and left Sheremetyevo on the same day.
Yesterday, the lawyer of the American Anatoly Kucherena said that his client got to work in a “major Russian company”.
On the surveillance of the NSA for Angela Merkel last week said the German media.
According to reporters, the Americans began to be listened to telephone conversations of the Chancellor back in the year of 2002.
Surveillance station allegedly stayed in the U.S. Embassy in Berlin.
The authorities of Germany after the publication of this information stated that the surveillance of Merkel violates the laws and sovereignty of Germany.
