Ben Grinman: The Tenth Anniversary of the New York Comedy Festival
We can safely say that New York is the place of the birth of a stand-up comedy in America: almost a hundred years ago, the pop artist Frank Faye, who worked as an entertainer at the Palace Theatre on Broadway, began to tell jokes directly to the public in a colloquial manner.
Fei’s innovation was eventually picked up by others, most recently – the New York comedy festival.
Created and overseen by Caroline Hursch, the founder of Carolines stand-up club, is celebrating its 10th Anniversary this year, numbering more than sixty shows in small clubs and large theaters.
“Most of these headliners started in Carolines, and then achieved more success, reached the level when they are too famous to perform at the club,” Hörsh said.
We’ve created this festival as a way to continue working with them.
This year’s event features Wanda Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Mare, and “Stendap for Heroes” – an annual musical and comedy charity for military veterans at Madyson Square Garden, where Bruce Springston, John Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby appear among others.
With the enlargement of the festival, the world of comedy has also widened.
Several comedians participating in the festival this year have gained popularity unconventional, for example thanks to a show on small TV channels like Comedy Central, FX and Spike.
Nick Kroll gained notoriety thanks to his role in the comedy series on a little-known cable channel (deliberate scabratory “League” on FXX on the topic of fantasy football), and now hosts its own sketch show at Comedy Central.
Jenny Slate entered the cast in both “Saturday Night Live” and “Park and Recreation”, although she is most famous for a series of viral videos “Marseille is a raspberries in shoes”.
Both Kcroll and Slate, like other young comedians with an intrinsic style (a surretically pessimistic Entonie Jesselnik, sluggish, foliage-footed Kamau BALL), is the result of a decentralized world of American comedy.
One of the brightest moments of the festival will be David Steinberg’s interview with Larry-Davi.
Steinberg began as a stand-up comedian, but then became a successful director on television and in the movies, as well as an unofficial comedy historian.
From from 2005 to 2007, he was the host of the show on TV Land called Sit Down Comedy with David Steinberg.
The meeting will take place at the Town Hall in the center of Manhattan.
