The government began negotiations with the local thalibans
The Pakistani authorities began negotiations with the Taliban.
This was reported by the Embassy of this country in the UK, where Pakistani Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif is on a visit.
The Prime Minister of Pakistan made a statement about the negotiations with Islamic extremists during a meeting with the British Deputy Prime Minister Nick Clegg, with whom he discussed issues of relations between Pakistan and India, the global energy market and economic reforms in Pakistan.
“The prime minister reported that the dialogue with the “Taliban” movement has begun”, – said in a statement the embassocating Embassy of Pakistan in London.
- He said that he hopes and prays for the dialogue to work in accordance with the constitution of Pakistan,” the diplomats said.
The Pakistani Talipses (Tehrik-i-Taliban Pakistan) are allies of the eponymous Afghan movement, but they operate independently of it in the north-western regions of the country bordering with Afghanistan.
The Pakistani thalis are seeking the creation of an Islamic state.
Earlier, the government of Islamabad made attempts to start negotiations with the local Taliban, but the movement renounced a dialogue.
It declared that it will negotiate only if the authorities will withdraw troops stationed in areas where the Talibs are operating, as well as release the arrested Islamists from prison.
Nawaz Sharif arrived in London for the World Islamic Economic Forum.
On Tuesday, October 29, the British Prime Minister David Cameron, the head of the Pakistan government and Afghan President Hamid Karzai held a trilateral meeting in London, which discussed the prospects for the peace process in Afghanistan.
