The court Suspended Execution of Judgment on ‘Stop-Seating’ at New York City Police
On Thursday, the federal district court of appellate suspended the execution of a court order demanding changes in the “stop-scary” program of the New York police department, and dismissed the judge from the case.
The U.S. court of appeal of the Second Circuit stated that the execution of the decisions of Judge Shira Sheindlin will be postponed until the results of an appeal filed by the city will be received.
In August, the judge decreed that the city had violated the constitution in how he carried out a program for street detention and interrogations.
The city lodged an appeal against its decision and decisions on corrective measures, including an ordinance to designate a controller to assist the police office to change the practice of work and its related training programme.
On Thursday, an appellate appellate court heard arbitrariness of the parties at the request to suspend the case.
The court of corresoppés said it is necessitating the judge due to the fact that she violated the code of conduct of US judges and did not consider it necessary to avoid a demonstration of bias, partly due to a series of interviews in the media and official statements that contain a public response to criticism of the court.
The judge ruled that police officers violated the civil rights of tens of thousands of people unreasonably appealing to black and Hispanics under the “stop-and-search program”.
It appointed an external controller to overwhelm large-expenditure, which included reforms in the field of policies, training and control, and also decided to launch a pilot program to test video cameras anchored in those police stations where most of the detentions take place.
In August, New York came to the decision to stop storing the names and addresses of people who were terminated after a co-examination.
Debates on the city's appeal are due to take place after March 14, 2014.
Tactics “stop and search” were criticized by many civil rights lawyers.
“Storage-search” has been practiced for several decades in one way or another, however, the number of documented stops has increased dramatically under the administration of independent Mayor Michael Blomberg, reaching an absolute record of 684 330 in 2011, the majority of which are black and Hispanics.
In 2004, four people, all representatives of national minorities, filed a collective lawsuit.
Contributors to changes in the “stop-and-rearge” program at the New York Police Department say these changes will put an end to unscrupulous practices, form a more reliable and effective policing service, and may influence other police departments implementing this practice.
Opponents argue that the changes will reduce the morale of the police, not the level of crime, will lead to unnecessary spending of money and will not solve a more global problem of difficult conditions in the police due to a decrease in the number of officers by several thousand in the last decade.
The judge noted that she does not cease the practice of “stop-and-search”, which corresponds to the constitution, but is changing the way how the New York police stops people.
