The report also provides a general picture of the discrimination faced by interseciliar and transgender people in the field of employment, as well as the level of harassment, atrocities and crimes of prejudice.
Kids with gender-defendants now have a special camp.
Australas and Nepal are already allowing adults to put a masculine, female or “three sex” in their official documents.
In June, the 52-year-old resident of Australia, Norry May-Welby, became the first person in the world recognized as “silence”, defending in court the right to maintain “uncertificated” sexual status for a lifetime.
According to the Ministry of Internal Affairs, a third designation will be used in German passports, in addition to the M and F-X, i.e. intersex.
In neighboring France, gender issues still cause contradictions, according to a news release on France 24.
In 2011, dozens of legislators from France, this pronounced Catholic country, signed a petition to seize "gender theory" from school textbooks.
The American website Catholic Online also expressed disagreement with the German law, writing that “in a situation when the world is drawn into a new state, where the sex is a matter of choice, and sexual activism is not, we turn over two more pillars of civilization.”
One mother of a newborn child from Maryland also told the Baby Zone website that she would have conceived for the children to be installed by gender at birth.
“It’s hard to raise a child and without additional restrictions, especially if you don’t know your child’s sex,” she told a website for her parents.
Kids need stability and confidence.
Historically, children born with masculine and feminine germaphrodites were called hermaphrodites - by the name of a beautiful Greek god, who possessed the signs of both sexes.
And only ten years ago the medical community perceived the germangear as a sheet on which everything can be erased, and then drawn again.
But now many are contesting the ethical grounds for surgery, knowing that gender identity is complex, and the doctors sometimes make an incorrect choice, not knowing how the child will perceive the gender assigned to him, when they grow.
“In the mid-twentieth century, it was called an ‘urresponsible psychiatric case’,” Drescher said.
When such children were born, you didn’t call a psychiatrist—you called a surgeon.
Dominant theory about how to deal with children with intermediate-type genitals was proposed by Dr. John Mani from Johns Hopkins University, who believed that the gadner could easily be changed.
He introduced the term “gender Identity” and argued that environmental signals, including those of the social – how parents raise a child – interact with a child’s genes and hormones, declaring whether a person will consider himself a man or a woman.
But in a 1966 case known as "Jone/Joan", his theories were in question.
He advisited the parents of the boy, whose penis was injunction during neighbabilly circumcision, completely castrate the child, removing his testicles, and gravitating him like a girl.
“Mani presented the case as a successful example of the transition, but that wasn’t the case,” Drescher said.
When the boy was about 15 years of age, he again became a boy and married a woman.
But at the age of 38, he committed suicide.
Dresher said some doctors are still “applying this model now.”
But in the 1990-ies, with the advent of the Internet, people who survived these sex resonance operations, declared themselves “dissatisfied with the result”.
This was the case of Jim Bruce, a 36-year-old writer from Montana, who was born with male chromosomes of XY, but intermediary-type genitals.
The doctors couldn’t pinpoint whether he had a large clitoris or a small penis, and were convinced that he would never be able to live a “full-fledged life”.
Therefore, shortly after his birth in 1976, the external sexual and testicles of Bruce were surgically removed, and he was brought up as a girl.
He began to give female hormones at the age of 12.
"I knew I wasn't a girl," he told ABCNews.com
I was unlucky, but it was very difficult to ask questions.
At the age of 18, waginoplasty was prescribed.
But being dismayed and realizing that something is wrong, he demanded medical documentation.
What he found out was terrible.
I was sterilized at birth, and no one has ever told me about it.
Bruce was born with impaired sexual development, which did not allow his body to produce enough testosterone for the normal development of the genitals.
Having recognized the truth, he again became a man, receiving testosterone shots and removing his chest.
Surgery made it infertile.
Today he speaks to others in an organization called the Interface Project, attempting to normal the perception of people with interference.
But Anne Temar-Mattits, Executive Director of the California Law Association Advocates for Informed Choice, is concerned that German law “encourages labeling and stigma.”
“Many activists are concerned that the German law will encourage their parents to make hasty decisions and give a ‘uncertificated sex,’” she said.
We are afracating that it will facilitate the intervention.
We believe that it is better to assign a male or a feminine gender, and then wait.
But we haven’t yet seen how this law will manifest ourselves, so everything we can do is make assumptions.
Teamard-Mattis said that her organization supports Australian law, because “it allows adult people to make a choice in favor of the third sex.”
“Advipared people should be able to make their own decisions about formal sex,” she says.
The German law implies its ascribing at birth.
This is a battle in which young children should not enter at this stage.
When they grow up, then they will be able to make decisions about their own body.
But Dr. Arlyne Baratz, a psychologologist from Pittsburgh who has a diligible daughter with impaired sexual development and who helps hundreds of other people in the support group, said that the German law would “substitute” for both parents and children.
Daughter of Barats, Katie, was born with male chromosomes, but she has impaired sexual development under the name “a syndrome complete insensitivity for androgens”.
Since her androgenic androgenic receptacles are working incorrectly, Katie has developed feminine traits.
She has a vagina, but no uterus or ovaries.
At the age of 29, Katie is marriable and residency in Child Psychiatry at Pennigrian University.
Although she is infertile, she hopes to become a parent through adoption or gestating surrogacy.
“The law gives parents time not to make hasty decisions themselves,” Baratz said.
He gives them the time to do some tests and understand the situation, as well as a period of time before they write “male” or “female”.
In this case, you are in the quench - raising a child, loving a child.
You have a wonderful child, and you feel joy.
We don't have to have an unreversable operation.
"This allows children to participate in a solution and eliminates excitement that motivates their parents, because they don't feel like they're doing it right," she said.
In the end, the child will decide what sex he or she is more comfortable with - and this is wonderful.
This gives the children the opportunity to make a decision on their own.
