Early puberty: aging earlier
Afro-Has American and Hispanic girls usually reach puberral maturity earlier than their white peers, research shows.
Physical changes do not implicate pneumonia close
There is no testimonial evidence to blame for hormones or other chemicals
Experts believe that the epidemic of obesity can be one of the starting mechanisms of early puberty
The supremacy for early puberty is not so pronounced
Former CNN correspondent Pat Eterij is a journalist who specializes in children's health and family issues.
Should the mother be fascinated if her daughter began to form breast rudiments and pubic hair grow at the age of 7 or 8 years?
At an annual conference of the American Pediatric Academy of Pediatricia this week in Orlando, Florida pediatric endocrinologist Dr. Paul Caplovitz explained that these early physical changes are quite common among American girls and represent a new norm.
“I spend a lot of time reassuring my parents – it usually doesn’t mean the rapid onset of complete puberty,” Caplovitz said.
Evidentiary signs of development, such as growing breast, puppet and auxiliary hair and body vein, appear earlier in girls.
But there has only been a small displacement at the anniversary of menarch (first menstruation) for the last four decades.
In the U.S., an average age is 12.5 years and has dropped from 12.75 in 1970.
“Once the breasts begin to develop, at least two or a year before menarche passes,” said Caplovitz, who is also the author of “Epair puberty in girls: how to cope with this common problem.”
The time is the most accurate test of how puberty will progress.
There is a discussion about what is the actual onset of sexual maturity, but it is considered "premature", when an enlargement of the breast is accompanied by a sharp increase in growth to 8 years.
In most cases, the process will slow down or sank - it's that the pediatrician can track carefully.
A faster progression can serve as a basis for tests in an endocrinologist in order to exclude serious problems, such as tunals or cysts.
There are procedures to decipher early menstruation and prevent another consequence: premature bone agriation, which can ultimately lead to a divergence of stimulus and low growth in speculative age.
Recommendations for medicinal or hormonal therapy are based on a child's age, level of development, growth rate and emotional maturity.
Psychosocial aspects are also important.
Caplovlitz is careful with medications, but admits that “suppression of puncturation can soften the behavioral problems and sensations of the girls that they are different from their peers.”
Another big problem is understandable: the parents simply do not want their very young dyes to have menstruation.
“They’re worrying about the risk of pregnancies, or even how they deal with hygiene issues,” Caplovitz said.
“It’s been a shock,” recalls one woman, whose daughter’s menstruation began at the age of 10.
Although there were tramps and we were talking about menstruation, she wasn’t emotionally ready.
She came to her home from school fictitious and upset that she was the first among her friends.
There’s a lot of propagated theories about the causes of early puberty.
Nevertheless, there is no sequential evidence that hormones are to blame in milk or other foodstuffs, chemicals in the environment, or sexual intermittances in the media.
In boys - as well as in girls - puberty occurs earlier.
Caplovlitz argues the prematurity, which has the greatest weight, is an epidemic of obesity.
In the year of 2001, he helped to conduct a study of girls from 6 to the age of 9, which associates the amount of fat in the body with punctuation periods.
Other data confirm this conclusion, but there are a lot of other contributing factors.
In this country, Afro-American and Hispanic girls, as a rule, reach sexual magnificence earlier than their white peers.
There are various explanations.
Around the world, early puberty seems to be affected by everything from economic conditions and climate to genes.
Still a ridicule: although in the boys hair on the floes appear at a younger age, the tendency to complete early puberty is not as pronounced as in girls.
Other doctors participating in the AAP conference, confirmed the complexity of this topic.
The appearance of pimples and pubic hair is found even in infants and children of torn age.
“We need to be careful about how we determine the true onset of puberty,” said Dr. Lawrence Silverman, a pediatric endocrinologist at Gorieb Children's Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Elaborative parents should not be embarrassed to receive instructions from the pediatrician on how to talk to their child.
“This could mean an earlier-than-expected conversation,” Caplovlitz explained.
If you remain calm, your child will usually react well.
Girls who blossom early, need confirmation that even when this happens earlier, this process is a normal part of life.
