We need further tests prior to human trials, which will take several years, but Professor Ronald Crystal from Walell Cornell Medical Medical College in New York said that the first results are encouraging.
“We very much reliate that such a vaccine application strategy can finally help the millions of smokers who have tried to stop by trying all the methods available on the market today, but found that their nicotine dependencies are quite strong enough to overcome it with these modern means,” Cornell said.
The new Vaccine Contains Harmless Virus, which was designed to carry genetic information to produce anti-nicotine antiplates.
The virus selectively indicates liver cells, which then begin to produce a stable flow of antibodies.
Antibodies close any nicotine molecules in the blood, neutralizing them before they reach the brain, preventing a nicotine stroke by a smoker from obtaining nicotine.
In tests, vaccinated muses who were subsequently given nicotine continued their normal activities.
And the mice that weren’t given the vaccinated “were relaxed”, and it’s a sign that nicotine has come to their cerebral,” the researchers said.
These experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
previous tobacco vaccines failed because they contained antibodies.
In order to maintain a high level of antibodies, the injections had to be done so often that they turned out to be expensive and impractical.
But the cost of the new vaccine is likely to be much lower, because it turns liver cells into antibody factories.
Professor Crystal said that if the future human vaccine were completely safe, it could be given to children before they wanted to try a cigarette, preventing nicotine dependency.
But it’s more likely to be used by smokers to stop smoking.
“They will know that if they start to smoke again, they won’t enjoy it because of nicotine vacation, and it can help them get rid of that habit,” he said.
British scientists said that the results were interesting, but warned that additional research is needed.
