The rules for the use of electronic devices on aircraft in Australia for the time will remain in force
Passengers of Australian airlines will need to continue to turn off their tablets and smartphones during take-off and landing, regardless of measures in the United States to soften the rules regarding these devices.
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration allowed U.S. carriers to change their procedures in such a way that the passengers can read e-books, watch videos or play games on their devices during take-off and landing, as long as they remain in “Travel Mode”.
Passengers can already do this during the main part of the flight, but it annoyed to many that they do not have access to their e-books during take-off and landing.
“Australian carriers are looking at this decision, demanding from U.E. airlines to carry out a huge amount of work to meet the new requirements, but indicated that they do not plan in the near future to change their procedures in the near future.
The Civil Aviation Aviation Safety Office also noted that they are considering this claim, but stressed that restrictions on the use of electronic devices in critical flight phases are still valid in Australia.
“Civil aviation safety management does not currently have special rules governing the use of electronic devices on airplanes,” he said.
This issue is regulated by the rules, which require air carriers to maintain safety at any possible time, and passengers to comply with the safety rules, which the crew members report to them.
The company Virgin, which has already been accustomed with the Aviation Safety Safety Administration, the expansion on board of its entertainment system with Wi-Fi, was not against the changes, but said that it will follow the instructions of the regulator.
“We’d welcome consideration by the Civil Aviation Aviation Safety Administration about allowing the use of electronic devices, because we’re really conceivable that this will improve the quality of customer service now that it’s on our planes,” the spokeswoman said.
Qantas said that it will adhere to the current rules.
“Our current policy lies in the fact that electronic devices can not be used during take-off and landing, and in the near future we do not plan to change it,” she said.
The FAU ruling applies to U.S. airlines.
Nevertheless, we are always interested in regulatory changes that could benefit the passengers, and we, of course, will carefully study the decision of the FAU and the reasons that caused it.
For U.S. carriers, the influence of the ruling will range from the airline and will depend on the age of their fleet.
Carriers will have to prove that their aircraft are sanctified for radio interference from mobile devices, as well as to revise manuals, training materials, norms for the transportation of hand luggage and instructions for passengers.
“Once the airline checks and confirms the immunity of its navy, it can allow passengers to use portable, light electronic devices, such as tablets, e-books and smartphones, at any altitude,” she said.
In rare cases of poor visibility, the crew will give instructions to the passengers to turn off their devices during the landing.
In addition, according to the group's recommendation, heavier devices must be positioned under the seats or in the upper luggage compartments during take-off and landing.
