Pawnshop Owners Are Doing Good Describing In Singapore While the Middle Grade Is Difficulty
In a puploble at the Bendemere shopping marctill in Singapore, Gianani Amirthhaling lays a golden bracelet, a ring and an earring to plough for his daughter's school.
“My Husband and I have just bought a house, so I’ve invested my money in there,” says Ms. Amirthhaling.
Even though she earns S$3000 ($2400) a month as an administrator and her husband is also working, there isn’t a sufficient monthly family income, she says.
Indeed, the demand in various parts of South-Eastern Asia – where household accrual is growing – such that ValueMax, where it conducts its deal, became the third pawnshop listed on Singapore’s stock exchange this week.
Divorced Loans is not just a quick way to get money (S$1300 in the case of Madam Amirthhaling), but almost as cheap as unsecured bank loans.
Typically, phlugs in Singapore accrue an effective annual interest rate of 17% - just over 15.4% offered at United Overseas Bank, a local lender with a branch at the same shopping mall.
However, plombers have the advantage that they don’t require checking creditworthiness or salary evidence and can issue loans more quickly than banks.
Therefore, millions of people in the whole region apply to pawnshops, as families feel the pressure due to an increase in the cost of living and a rapid increase in household debt and consumer debt.
After a five-hung years of steady growth that followed the global financial crisis and cheap loans fed up with free monetary policies in advanced economies, low- and middle-income families go to pawnshops to offset income declines as their economies slowed down.
Standard & Poor's this week, the ratings agency, pointed an increase in family leverage, mainly due to rising mortgages, as a risk factor for the creditworthiness of Asian banks.
It stated that Malaysia, Thailand, and Singapore had the highest debt-debt-reliance of households to gross domestic product in Asia.
Malaysia topped this list with an increase of 80 per cent of GDP compared to 60 percent in 2008.
Economists are also concerned about the high level of consumer debt in Thailand, which this week barely came out of a technical regression.
On Thursday, the data showed a continuing decline in exports and a weakening of consumer indemnity.
“It’s the point is that with the rise in prices, people in the middle and lower [revencies scales] will look for ways to replenish their earnings wherever they can,” says Song Sung Wong Woon, an economist at CIMB, a Malaysian bank.
Record high gold prices in the last two years have intensified the haste with which people lay their personal belongings, as they use the opportunity to receive money for their family ornaments.
In Singapore, about 70 percent of the items included in 200 puploparts of city-of-the-greserve are gold products.
People say: “The price of the gold looks good, let’s put the grinder’s gold chain and get it back next month.”
In Thailand’s largest grootubling operator EasyMoney has observed an increase in the number of customers using its offices in recent months, up to 20 per cent.
Grow in the pawnshop business is such that ValueMax, an operator in Bendemere and 15 others in Singapore, plans to expand not only in neighboring Malaysia, where it has four stores, but also outside of Asia, says Yeh Lee Ching, Executive Director of ValueMax.
The company will finance the expansion, using 60 per cent of S$66 million raised this week as a result of the placement of shares on the Singapore Stock Exchange.
While some creditors have sparked criticism because of high interest rates, she said the pawnshop doesn’t just offer cheaper tariffs than other lenders, it doesn’t increase the debt directly.
“Customers are laying items that they already own, and monetization of personal assets doesn’t increase household arrears,” she says.
Balance is gaining increasing public recognition as a means of obtaining short-term, secured funding.
And at the same time puppeteers use not only those categories of people who are in a difficult financial situation.
Wealthy people in Singapore also use ValueMax sediments by planting gold ingots or Rolex watches, which can provide up to 60 per cent of their purchased value in cash.
We can see clients from all the segments of society.
“They include wealthy individuals who need to borrow money for a short period of time for commercial projects or investments, or a small-creature business that needs to overcome temporary monetary difficulties,” says Ms. Yeah.
“Sometimes they just need money very quickly.
