Salvation package in the amount of $ 325 million for Tasman’s health
The federal government is pushing for a $325 million rescue package for Tasmaniya’s weakened healthcare system will be complemented by stringent conditions, which ensure that the state’s government doesn't wasted.
The Federal Minister of health Tanya Plibersek announced that the Commonwealth is taking “urresponsive measures” to prevent the crisis caused by an aging population of this island state, a higher level of Chronic diseases and system restrictions.
The decision on financing for a four-year period was made after consultations of the government with the independent Deputy of Tasmania Andrew Wilkey.
“The government has proposed a package of emergency rescue measures that, we believe, will solve the specific problems facing the staff,” Plibersek said today.
The $325 million package includes $31 million per blitz-campaign on planned transactions.
Another 2600 operations, including orthopedic and cataract removal operations, will help overcome the backlog.
There is also a lot of money for clinics accepting patients unreleased in Hobart and Lonencesone to improve the quality of post-in-patient care, training of medical professionals, mental health services and the introduction of personal electronic medical records systems in local hospitals.
“These investments will go on the best-studies to implement ideas on how best to help the Tasmanian health care system,” Plibersek said.
The Minister insisted that the Tasmanian government will work in the mode of strict accountability and responsibilities.
The state will need to maintain the current levels of financing in order to receive Commonwealth funds, and to report monthly on where it spends these additional funds.
A commission of three people will be set up to make sure the state provides services as efficiently and more efficiently as possible.
Wilky said today that $325 million will not solve anything, “if they are not followed by a true reform, which will put Tasmania’s health system on a more sustainable basis.”
Nevertheless, he commended the government for responding to his request for urgent assistance he first addressed the prime minister in early May.
"I hope that the federal aid package will significantly contribute to the removal of this state's health system out of a critical state," Wilkey said.
According to the state government, these additional sampled procedures largely offset recent cuts.
