FAU: "Air passengers can now use gadgets on airplanes (but do not call from a mobile phone)
Passengers will be able to use their electron devices throughout the flight to read, work, play games, watch movies, and listen to music, but do not talk on mobile phones – according to the long-awaited new guidelines, published on Thursday by the Federal Aviation Administration.
Passengers shouldn’t expect the changes happen immediately.
How quickly such a change is realized will vary depending on the airline, said FAA administrator Michael Huerta at a press conference.
Aviad companies will have to show FAA how their aircraft comply with the new guiding principles and that they are renovating their training manuals for flight crew members and rules for styling devices in order to comply with the new guiding principles.
FAU stated that it has already received plans from some of the airlines to expand the use of portable electronic devices in airplanes.
Delta and JetBlue were among the airlines, which have already presented plans.
“Decimium on the state of the plan, we could have approved an increased use of electronic devices very soon soon,” she said.
Currently, passengers are obligated to turn off their smartphones, tablets and other devices, as soon as the plane’s door closes.
They don't have to restart them until the aircraft reaches a height of 10 000 feet and the captain does not give "good".
Passengers must turn off their devices again, when the aircraft drops to land, and do not turn them on until the plane is on the ground.
In accordance with the new guiding principles, airlines whose planes are duly protected from electronic interference may allow passengers to use these devices during take-off, boarding and shielding.
Most of the new airliners and other aircraft, modified in such a way so that passengers can use Wi-Fi at high altitudes, are expected to meet these criteria.
Laura Glaiding, president of the Association of professional flight attendants, welcomed these changes.
“Since the new policy will be successfully implemented (and we are going to work in close contact with the carrier in order to achieve this), it will be a win-win option,” Gleyding said.
We are frankly overwhelmed to feel “chosen on-creational jobs” when it comes to this issue.
But an Internet connection for surfing, exchanging e-mails, text messages, or downloading of data will still be banned at an altitude of less than 10 000 ft, the agency said.
Passengers will be invited to switch their smartphones, tablets and other devices to an airplane mode.
Thus, there is still no “Words with friends” – an intellectual Internet game in which actor Alec Baldwin played on his smartphone in 2011, when there was an accumulative case: the actor was removed from an American Airlines plane for refusing to turn off the device at a time when the plane was on landing.
And heavier devices, such as laptops, will still need to be placed in the luggage compartment, since they can injure someone if they will fly throughout the cabin.
Calls from the cell phone in flight will also continue to be banned.
Regulatory credentials on phone calls belong to the Federal Communications Commission, and not FAU.
FAU can lift the ban on some electronic devices during take-off and landing
Last month, Mark Rosenker of the National Transportation Safety Board, CBS News, said cell phones are still considered a risk.
“Cell phones are really a problem, not only just because they can potentially interfere with navigation devices, but we’re aware of FCS that they can interfere with cell towers when the plane is in the air,” Rosenker said.
An industry advisory committee established by the FAU to study this issue has recommended last month that the government authorize the increased use of personal electronic devices.
In recent years, there has been an increasing pressure on FAA in order to easing restrictions on their use.
Critiques, such as Missouri Democrat Senator Claire McKasickel, argue that there are no good reasons related to security.
Compliance with restrictions is also becoming increasingly difficult to provide, since the use of devices has become ubiquitous.
Some studies show that up to one-a-half passengers are forgetting or ignoring instructions to turn off their devices.
FAU began to restrict the use of electronic devices by passengers in 1966 in response to messages about interference for navigation and communication equipment, when passengers began to carry FM-receipters - high-tech gadgets at that time.
New airliners are much more advisable on electrical systems than previous generations of aircraft, but they are also being developed and approved by the FAU as resistant to electronic interference.
The airlines have been provivinating the use of Wi-Fi at crutary height for several years.
Airplanes Modified for Wi-Fi systems are also terrified of interference.
The vast majority of liners are adapted for wider use of electron devices according to the new guiding principles, Huerta said.
Current electronic devices usually emit radio signals of much lower power than previous generations of devices.
Devices for reading electronic books, for example, emit only minimal signals when pages flipping.
But signals become more potable when devices upload or send data.
Among those who sought to easolate restrictions on the use of electronic devices by passengers was Amazon.
In 2011, representatives of the company uploaded an entire airliner with their devices to read the Kindle e-book and Flew it to verify the presence of problems, but did not reveal them.
The members of the Advisory Committee expressed mitigating feelings as to whether the use of the devices posed a risk.
Douglas Kidd of the National Association of Air Passengers said that he considers interference from the devices to be real, even if the risk is minimal.
Other members of the committee said that there are only separate reports of the pilots confirming that the devices can interfere with aviation systems, and the majority of these messages are very old.
Nevertheless, the committee has recommended that FAU allow pilots to require passengers to disauggle the devices while landing on devices in conditions of limited visibility.
A group of representatives of the tourism industry welcomed these changes, calling them a sensible step towards passengers, who now use a lot of technological devices.
“We are delighted that the FAU recognizes that passenger comfort isn’t incompatible with safety and protection,” said Roger Dow, Director-general of the U.S. Tourism Association.
Driving schools will be able to carry out exams for obtaining a driver's license
Examinations for a driving license will be accepted by private companies that have their own computerized Circuit.
