Is the elite of Europe ready to do business with England?
The business for Britain campaign was launched in April with an obligation to assemble business representatives and determine what changes the UK’s Welfare and Welfare-Hacking Makers Want to See in Relations with the European Union.
To this end, we have commissioned the largest and most comprehensive survey of British business leaders, asking them to express their thoughts about the UK, business and the EU.
YouGov has interviewed more than 1000 business leaders, representing a wide slice of business in the U.K. in size, sectors and regions.
Conclusions of the survey will be a surprise for many.
We have found that the vast majority of enterprises are currently looking for export opportunities beyond Europe, paying special attention to countries that carry out modernizing and growing, while EU countries are stabbing.
They want the government to prioritize new trade relations with countries such as China, India and Brasilia, rather than wasting its time on a long and difficult process of reforming confusing EU institutions.
When they were asked about their views on specific political issues, from antimonopoly regulation to product laws, the majority of business leaders believed that the control of these key competencies should be returned to Westminster.
General dissatisfaction was attended by a single market, and representatives of the business stated that the costs of regulation from Brussels now outweigh the advantages of staying in the trading zone of the European trade zone - even 40 percent of large enterprises, traditionally the most pro-European-minded of all companies, agreed with this.
Latter-industrious and most indicative – our survey of business executives found that a probate majority wants the UK to pursue a course to change the contract and on relations based on trade, rather than on politics.
This result, which was observed among enterprises of all sizes and largest business groups, shows that the business insists on a “measurable change”, which will return the authority back to the United Kingdom.
Bets are high - achieving changes in the contract and more favorable conditions for the UK will provide a 16 percent shift in the vote to remain in the EU.
The prime minister should not have any indifference: this poll shows that the British business supports his plan to revise the terms of Britain’s membership in the European Union.
It also shows that the business expects that the revision will lead to a significant shift in the current balance of power back towards the UK.
The best conditions for the British business are possible and become more and more necessary as the eurozone embarks on the path of a closer economic and financial association.
Priority should be given to jobs and economic growth in the United Kingdom, and, as the results of our survey show, for business this means an increasing focus on trade and a fundamental change in the normative activities of Brussels.
