Scholars shed light on how the dog’s tail movements are associated with its intuition.
Earlier studies have shown that satisfied dogs wag their tail in the right direction (from a dog's point of view), while anxious dogs wave their tail mainly on the left.
But now scientists are saying that other representatives of the dog family may notice these subtle differences in wagging with a tail and react to them.
Professor Giorgio Vallortigar, a neuroscientist from the University of Trent, said: “We are well-knowledge that people’s left and right-to-right sides of the brain are involved in the shaping of motivators who cause positive or negative emotions.”
Here we tried to look at it in other species.
He added that, just like a person, in dogs the right part of the brain controls the left side of the body and vice versa, and two hemispheres play different roles in emotions.
To find out more about how the dogs respond to versataneous whirlwinding of other dog’s tails, the researchers looked at the animals as they watched movies about other dogs.
They measured the frequency of animal heartbeats and analysed their behavior.
It’ll probably soon see why their lofts are sometimes directed to one side, and sometimes to the other
Professor Vallortigara said: “We’ve shown the dogs video with dogs—either naturalistic versions or silhouette to get rid of any other distorting factors, and we’ve been able to manipulate the tail and direct the tail more to the left or right.”
When the animals saw that in the rest of annoying dog moved the tail to the right (from the point of view wheeling tail of the dog), they remained completely completely relaxed.
But when they noticed that the tail leined mainly to the left (again from the point of view of a dog wagging tail), their heartbeat increased and they looked anxietious.
Professor Vallortigara said that he does not think that the dogs deliberately communicated to each other with these movements.
In fact, he believes that the dogs have learned from their own experience, what movements they should worry about, and about which they do not.
He notes: “If there are several encounters with other dogs and often wilting their tail in one direction is associated with a more accommodating behavior, and the right-side gives less-to-complicated behavior, then the dog reacts on the basis of this experience.”
Agencies say these results can give owners, veterinarians and trainers a more in-depth understanding of their animal’s emotions.
The expert in the field of dog behavior John Bradshaw, a visiting researcher at Bristol University veterinary veterinary school, said that this was not the first study to check whether the left and right sides are important for dogs.
Last year, a group of scientists from the University of Lincoln found that the dogs turn their heads to the left, if you look at an aggressive dog, and to the right, if they look at a happy dog.
And in another scientific work of the University of Victoria in Canada, he reported: “Dogs more often approached a robotic dog, when wilting its tail was headed to the left, not to the right, instead of getting anxious – the opposite behavior in relation to an Italian study.”
He said that the differences may be due to the fact that dogs in various studies did not fully perceive animals in films or robot dogs as dogs.
It could be a useful study of how dogs react to real dogs, he explained.
“While there’s a sufficient testimonial for different magnificers that two hemispheres of the brain are used for different purposes, many details have yet to be refined,” he said.
However, taking into account the frivolity from which their behavior can be recorded, we will probably soon understand why their tails are sometimes directed in one direction, and sometimes to the other.
Arctic Monkeys postpone concert in Glasgow because of the illness of Alex Turner
