GM Recalls a number of new pickups in the U.S. to retrieve the back of the seats
General Motor's Co recalls almost 19 thousand completely new Chevrolet Silverado pickups and GMC Sierra models of 2014 to eliminate problems with manually adjusting the backbone of the seats, in accordance with the notification presented on Friday from U.S. Regulatory Agencies in the field of vehicle safety.
On some of the pickups, the anterior seats can have a defect in the tilt of the back tilt mechanism.
As a result, the back of the seats does not comply with federal vehicle safety standards for head restraints.
“When hitting the car from the back, the head restraint may not adequately protect the driver and passengers, increasing the risk of injuring,” it is said in the website of the National Road Traffic Safety Administration website.
Responsible models were manufactured in the period from August 1 to September 10.
The production of GM pickup pumps began in June and represents the most important mass-produced launch of the car for the leading American automaker since its restructuring due to bankruptcies in 2009.
GM informed the owners of pickup trucks about the defect in the first half of October.
The National Road Traffic Safety Administration was unable to consider notifying holders in connection with a 16-day shutdown, which lowered car sales growth in October.
Sales of Silverado and Sierra pickups, which were upgraded for the 2014 model range, rose about 20 per cent during the first 10 months of this year, GM said on Friday.
In October, GM sold 42,660 Silverado pickup pickups and 16 503 Picopa Sierra.
GM's shares on the New York Stock Stock Exchange on Friday afternoon rose 1.4 per cent to $37.7.7.
