Informant individuals last month said RBS had handed over E-mail records and insane messages that a former trader received and was being sent by a former trader, the UK’s Financial Supervisory Authority (FCA).
This trade trader, Richard Usher, withdrew from RBS in 2010 and, as far as you know, he was granted a vacation in his current position as the head of European spot currency trading at JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, the head of European spot trading in Citi, went on vacation this week, while Matt Gardiner, the former senior currency trader at Barclays and UBS, was suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
None of these traders were accused of any violations.
Mr. Asher’s group of contacts included bankers from Barclays and Citigroup, according to informed individuals.
UBS reported this in a statement this week that it has taken action against some of its collaborators after the Swiss regulator Finma said it was investigating the alleged manipulation of the foreign exchange market in a number of Swiss banks.
At least six regulatory bodies in the world – the European Commission, Finma, Switzerland’s anti-monopoly body Weko, FCA, U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority — are considering allegations that bankers have conspired to influence the foreign exchange market.
HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse also launched internal audits or received inquiries about providing information from regulators, the individuals familiar with the situation said.
Banks are blossoming instant messages and e-mails for a few-threpth years to search for cases of offenses.
News about the audits stirred traders in the field of activity, which in recent years was one of the largest sources of income of the trading units of investment banks, which this year, however, faced with difficulties due to the fact that low currency volatility reduces favorable opportunities for resouppants.
Some bankers tried to downplay the severity of the situation, saying it is almost impossible to manipulate the vast and high-cliquid foreign exchange market, but older traders say this isn’t always right.
According to the senior trader, despite a huge volume of daily trading in the foreign exchange market, the fragmentation of liquidity between different trading platforms and the increased use by banks of their internal platforms means that “you can start influencies on the market at a very small price.”
This news came on the same day when Credit Suisse announced that it had dismissed one of the traders of his London Stock Exchange this week, after causing losses of almost $ 6 million at the end of last year.
Immediately notified the relevant authorities and cooperates with its regulators.
“We are sure that the trader acted alone and that this issue managed to be localized,” Credit Suisse said.
The Supreme court supported Obama’s law on healthcare
The U.S. Supreme Judgment today ruled that Barack Obama’s illegitimate health law is a constitutional one, which is a major victory for the Obama administration.
Having voted 5 against 4, the judges ruled that separate orders under the Patient Protection and Affordable Health Act, demanding that citizens purchase health insurance before 2014 or paid a fine, are constitutional in accordance with the authority of the government on taxation.
The President of the Supreme Judgment, John Roberts, joined the four-terrwinder of the court, while judges Scalias, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy were against.
The court also upheld the remaining sections of this 2700-page law and further ruled that the requirement of a health care law so that states should improve the compliance of the Medicaid program, otherwise they will lose all federal funding for the Medicaid program is unconstitutionally compulsive.
Claim for blocking the bill was filed by 26 states and the National Federation of independent business.
Against the law reluctegally opposed each of the main Republican presidential candidates during the final elections of 2012, including the alleged candidate Mitt Romney.
