At Flight Centre, there seems to be an inverse trend.
The company has revitalized its full-year earnings forecast and await a record revenue from vacationers in Australia and the United Kingdom.
This travel firm now expects that its main full-produced pre-tax profit will range from $325 to $340 million, compared to a previous forecast of $305 to $315 million.
If the current benchmarks are reached, this will amount to a 12-17% increase compared to a record profit of $ 290.4 million, received in 2011/2012.
Managing Director Graham Turner said Flight Centre received 8% of the profit in the first sequel in the first sememoria and the early second sequel with good performance, especially on non-business trips in Australia and the United Kingdom.
“During the period since the beginning of the year, we have been working with a profit for our 10 countries, and for some of them for a full-year it is expected a record-hosllant profit before interest and tax contributions,” he said.
“This includes Australia and Great Britain, which usually bring us the largest profits.”
“Australia’s leesh business has recovered in the second half of this year’s slightly weaker domestic corporate travel market.
Similarly, in the U.K, the Flight Centre’s Leisure business was going well, while corporate clients spent less.
Her U.S. business compensated for the losses incurred during the seasonal low-seaclifice of the first half and is expected to make a profit for the third year in a row.
Yesterday, the shares of the Flight Centre increased by 3 cents to $ 38.20.
